string a = "Test";
string b = "Test";

CLR does this:
	1.	Checks string intern pool
	2.	If "Test" already exists → reuse it
	3.	If not → create it and store in intern pool

So memory looks like:

Stack:                 Heap (Intern Pool):
------                 -------------------
a  ────────────────►   "Test"
b  ────────────────►   "Test"

Yes, wherever the literal value is same, they all point to one heap object (interned), not separate ones.

a += b does not mutate the string. It creates a new string instance and reassigns the reference.

a ───► "Test"
a += b;

Old: "Test"       (unchanged)
New: "TestTest"

a ───► "TestTest"

string a = new string("hello world".ToCharArray());
string b = new string("hello world".ToCharArray());

Because new explicitly tells CLR: “Create a new object.”
Interning only happens automatically for string literals, not for new string(...)

Intern pool exists to:
	•	Reduce duplicate string memory
	•	Improve performance
	•	Make reference comparisons faster

But CLR does NOT automatically intern dynamically created strings because:
	•	It costs lookup time
	•	Not always beneficial
	•	Could increase memory pressure


StringBuilder

When Is StringBuilder Better?

✅ Inside loops
✅ Large concatenations
✅ Unknown final size
✅ High-performance systems
✅ Logging frameworks
✅ JSON building

var sb = new StringBuilder();
for(int i = 0; i < 10000; i++)
{
    sb.Append(i);
}
string result = sb.ToString();

With string +=
Each step:
""
"0"
"01"
"012"
"0123"
Each is a new object

With StringBuilder
Buffer: [0][1][2][3]...
Just grows internal array when needed.
